    <header class="hero">
      <div class="title">
        <p class="eyebrow">Deep-space exploration</p><h1>
          Tomorrow, You’re Only a Day Away
        </h1>
      </div>
      <figure class="hero-photo">
        <img
          src="/images/blog/voyager-light-day/voyager-1-launch.jpg"
          width="1178"
          height="1500"
          fetchpriority="high"
          alt="Black-and-white photograph of the Titan IIIE–Centaur lifting off with Voyager 1 above the launch pad"
        /><figcaption>
          Voyager 1 leaves Cape Canaveral, September 5, 1977. <a
            href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/voyager-1-launch-1977/"
            >NASA/JPL-Caltech</a>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </header>
    <div class="layout">
      <nav class="contents" aria-label="Article contents">
        <p class="mono">Contents</p><ol>
          <li><a href="#journey">1. The 49-year day</a></li>
          <li><a href="#map">2. The map back home</a></li>
          <li><a href="#speed">3. How fast is fast?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#builders">4. Who’s building it?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#sources">Sources</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>
      <div class="article">
        <p class="dek">
          Voyager 1 took 49 years to reach a distance light covers in a day.
          What did we get for those years, and how many could we take off the
          next trip?
        </p>
        <p class="date mono">
          <time datetime="2026-11-18">November 18, 2026</time>
        </p>
        <p>
          At 2:16 a.m. Pacific on November 18, Voyager 1 became the first thing
          humans have made to reach one light-day from Earth. Send it a radio
          command at that distance and the signal gets there tomorrow. An
          immediate reply gets back about a day after that. The spacecraft
          needed 49 years and a little over two months to put that much space
          between us. <a
            href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/where-are-voyager-1-and-voyager-2-now/"
            >NASA’s mission tracker</a
          >.
        </p>
        <p>
          That’s 25.9 billion kilometers, or 173 times the average distance from
          Earth to the Sun. At Voyager’s current speed of about 17 kilometers
          per second relative to the Sun, it would cover the average Earth–Moon
          distance in a little over six hours. Let it run at that speed for a
          full year and it still covers only about 30 light-minutes. <a
            href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-1/"
            >NASA’s Voyager 1 mission history</a
          >.
        </p>

        <section id="journey">
          <h2>1. The 49-year day</h2>
          <p>
            NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory, run by Caltech, built both
            Voyagers in Pasadena. General Electric manufactured their computers
            to JPL specifications. Caltech physicist Ed Stone led the mission
            science as project scientist. The team built the spacecraft for a
            five-year mission to Jupiter and Saturn, with the planets lined up
            so that one close flyby could set up the next. Voyager 1 launched 16
            days after its twin and took a quicker route to Jupiter. <a
              href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/frequently-asked-questions/"
              >NASA spacecraft FAQ</a
            > · <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/fact-sheet/"
              >Mission fact sheet</a
            > · <a href="https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/ed-stone-trail/"
              >JPL’s Ed Stone history</a
            >.
          </p>
          <p>
            At Jupiter in 1979, Voyager 1 found a faint ring around the planet.
            Then JPL navigation engineer Linda Morabito noticed a crescent
            sticking out of the edge of Io in a picture she was using to check
            the spacecraft’s trajectory. It was a volcanic plume, the first
            active eruption ever seen beyond Earth. At Saturn in 1980, the probe
            revealed the fine structure of the rings and found Titan wrapped in
            a thick, nitrogen-rich atmosphere. The close pass by Titan sent
            Voyager 1 north of the plane of the planets. Voyager 2 took the
            route on to Uranus and Neptune. <a
              href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-1/"
              >Voyager 1’s encounters</a
            > · <a
              href="https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/images/pia00379-volcanic-eruptions-on-io/"
              >The image Morabito examined</a
            >.
          </p>
          <figure class="io-photo">
            <img
              src="/images/blog/voyager-light-day/io-volcanic-plume.jpg"
              width="800"
              height="800"
              alt="Voyager 1 image of Io showing a curved volcanic plume extending from the moon’s edge"
              loading="lazy"
            /><figcaption>
              Io on March 8, 1979. The plume off the limb gave away the presence
              of a volcano. <a
                href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/volcanic-eruptions-on-io/"
                >NASA/JPL</a
              >.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="timeline-figure">
            <div class="panel">
              <span class="figure-label mono">Figure 1</span><h3>
                Voyager 1’s journey from launch to one light-day
              </h3>
              <div class="metrics">
                <div>
                  <strong>49 years</strong><span>since launch</span>
                </div><div>
                  <strong>173 AU</strong><span>Earth-to-probe distance</span>
                </div><div>
                  <strong>48 hours</strong><span
                    >approximate radio round trip</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <ol class="timeline">
                <li>
                  <time>1977</time><span>Launches from Cape Canaveral.</span>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <time>1979</time><span
                    >Flies past Jupiter. Images reveal a ring and active
                    volcanism on Io.</span>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <time>1980</time><span
                    >Flies past Saturn and Titan, then heads out of the plane of
                    the planets.</span>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <time>1990</time><span
                    >Photographs Earth for the last time.</span>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <time>2012</time><span
                    >Crosses the heliopause into interstellar space.</span>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <time>2026</time><span
                    >Reaches one light-day from Earth.</span>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </div>
            <figcaption>
              Selected mission milestones. One astronomical unit (AU) is about
              149.6 million kilometers. <a
                href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-1/"
                >NASA mission history</a
              > · <a
                href="https://science.nasa.gov/resource/voyager-one-light-day-downloads/"
                >Light-day milestone</a
              >.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
          <p>
            Fourteen years of that journey have been spent outside the
            heliosphere, the bubble the solar wind blows into the surrounding
            space. Voyager 1 crossed its edge on August 25, 2012. A later burst
            of plasma oscillations let the team measure the density of its
            surroundings and confirm it had entered interstellar space. The
            boundary Voyager crossed marks where the solar wind gives way to the
            interstellar medium. The Sun’s gravity reaches much farther. <a
              href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/questions-and-answers-with-dr-ed-stone/"
              >NASA’s account of the crossing</a
            >.
          </p>
          <p>
            The clock is still running. Plutonium decay supplies heat to the
            probe’s radioisotope generators, and the electricity available for
            science and communications has dwindled. In September 2026 the team
            switched several systems to lower-power alternatives to extend the
            life of the remaining instruments. In October, Voyager went quiet
            for about 48 hours. A backup set of thrusters had come on
            automatically, and the signal returned before the team sent
            troubleshooting commands. At this distance, the spacecraft has to be
            able to take care of itself while its operators are still finding
            out what happened. <a
              href="https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/voyager/2026/10/29/nasas-voyager-1-conserves-power-resolves-brief-communications-pause/"
              >NASA’s October mission update</a
            >.
          </p>
          <figure class="photo-wide">
            <img
              src="/images/blog/voyager-light-day/pale-blue-dot.jpg"
              width="1600"
              height="1583"
              alt="A tiny pale dot representing Earth sits in a broad shaft of scattered sunlight against a dark background"
              loading="lazy"
            /><figcaption>
              Earth as seen by Voyager 1 on February 14, 1990, from roughly 6
              billion kilometers away. The dot takes up less than one pixel in
              the original image. Shown here in the 2020 reprocessing of the
              original data. <a
                href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/pale-blue-dot-revisited/"
                >NASA/JPL-Caltech</a
              >.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section id="map">
          <h2>2. The map back home</h2>
          <p>
            Bolted to each Voyager is a gold-plated copper record carrying
            sounds and images from Earth. One engraving on its cover is Frank
            Drake’s return address. Fourteen spokes point toward pulsars, the
            rotating remnants of dead stars whose beams sweep past us at
            measurable intervals. Those intervals are written in binary, using a
            transition in hydrogen to define the unit of time. A fifteenth line
            points toward the center of the Milky Way. The spokes are meant to
            locate the Sun, which gives a finder a solar system to look in. <a
              href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/golden-record-cover/"
              >NASA’s guide to the cover</a
            > · <a href="https://www.pbs.org/the-farthest/science/pulsar-map/"
              >How to read Drake’s map</a
            >.
          </p>
          <figure>
            <div class="diptych">
              <div>
                <img
                  src="/images/blog/voyager-light-day/golden-record-installation.jpg"
                  width="1050"
                  height="834"
                  alt="Engineers work on the Voyager spacecraft beside the round Golden Record cover"
                  loading="lazy"
                />
              </div><div>
                <img
                  src="/images/blog/voyager-light-day/golden-record-cover.jpg"
                  width="1000"
                  height="1000"
                  alt="Golden Record cover, with the starburst of pulsar directions engraved on the lower left"
                  loading="lazy"
                />
              </div>
            </div>
            <figcaption>
              Engineers install Voyager 1’s Golden Record cover in 1977. The
              starburst on the lower left of the cover is the pulsar map. <a
                href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/voyager-installing-the-golden-record/"
                >Installation, NASA/JPL-Caltech</a
              > · <a
                href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/golden-record-cover/"
                >Cover, NASA/JPL</a
              >.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
          <p>
            Some of the coordinates were rough even for a finder who reads
            binary. Robert Johnston’s reconstruction puts the pulsar J0953+0755
            at roughly 390 light-years based on the engraving’s scale. A later
            radio parallax measurement puts it around 850 light-years away.
            Another spoke, for J0826+2637, is off by about 18 degrees in its
            projected bearing relative to the galactic-center line in that
            reconstruction. The timing has an outlier too. Running the engraved
            periods backward gives dates clustered around 1970 for most of the
            pulsars. For J1645−0317, the same reconstruction yields a date more
            than three millennia before launch. Pulsars’ spin periods change
            over time, and that calculation assumes a steady slowdown. The
            engraved numbers describe the sky at a particular epoch, so a finder
            would have to account for how the clocks had changed. There is a
            clue for that too. A spot of uranium-238 on the cover acts as an
            independent clock. <a
              href="https://www.johnstonsarchive.net/astro/pulsarmap.html"
              >Johnston’s reconstruction</a
            > · <a href="https://doi.org/10.1088/0004-637x/755/1/39"
              >Radio parallax measurements</a
            > · <a
              href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/golden-record-cover/"
              >NASA’s description of the uranium clock</a
            >.
          </p>
          <p>
            Voyager’s own navigators use radio tracking from the Deep Space
            Network. The engraving anticipates a different approach for future
            missions. In 2017 NASA’s SEXTANT experiment on the International
            Space Station used the arrival times of X-ray pulses from four
            pulsars to estimate its own position. With an up-to-date timing
            model, a spacecraft can use the pulses as celestial clocks and work
            out where it is before anyone on Earth has heard from it. <a
              href="https://www.nasa.gov/centers-and-facilities/goddard/nasa-team-first-to-demonstrate-x-ray-navigation-in-space/"
              >NASA’s SEXTANT demonstration</a
            >.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="speed">
          <h2>3. How fast is fast?</h2>
          <p>
            Cutting Voyager’s time in half takes an average outward speed near
            33 kilometers per second. Getting there in a decade takes roughly
            82. A good departure gets you some of the way. A high-energy launch
            and the right Jupiter flyby can send a probe outward faster using
            familiar technology. A Johns Hopkins Applied Physics Laboratory
            study modeled an interstellar probe with an escape speed near 7 AU
            per year, almost twice Voyager’s current rate. That speed works out
            to about 25 years per light-day. The mission is a study, and the
            actual stopwatch would depend on the launch trajectory and time
            spent getting to Jupiter. <a
              href="https://spacephysics.princeton.edu/sites/g/files/toruqf1376/files/documents/brandt_2023.pdf"
              >Johns Hopkins APL’s Interstellar Probe study</a
            >.
          </p>
          <figure>
            <div class="panel">
              <span class="figure-label mono">Figure 2</span><h3>
                How long to cover one light-day?
              </h3>
              <div
                class="speed-chart"
                role="list"
                aria-label="Transit times across one light-day"
              >
                <div class="speed-row observed" role="listitem">
                  <span>Voyager 1<small>Actual elapsed time</small></span><span
                    class="rail"
                    aria-hidden="true"
                    ><span class="bar" style="--bar:99.69%"></span></span
                  ><strong>49.2 yr</strong>
                </div>
                <div class="speed-row" role="listitem">
                  <span
                    >7 AU/yr · 33 km/s<small>APL study cruise speed</small
                    ></span
                  ><span class="rail" aria-hidden="true"
                    ><span class="bar" style="--bar:86.48%"></span></span
                  ><strong>24.7 yr</strong>
                </div>
                <div class="speed-row" role="listitem">
                  <span>50 km/s<small>Illustrative cruise speed</small></span
                  ><span class="rail" aria-hidden="true"
                    ><span class="bar" style="--bar:78.61%"></span></span
                  ><strong>16.4 yr</strong>
                </div>
                <div class="speed-row" role="listitem">
                  <span>100 km/s<small>Illustrative cruise speed</small></span
                  ><span class="rail" aria-hidden="true"
                    ><span class="bar" style="--bar:65.30%"></span></span
                  ><strong>8.2 yr</strong>
                </div>
                <div class="speed-row" role="listitem">
                  <span
                    >1% of light speed<small>Illustrative cruise speed</small
                    ></span
                  ><span class="rail" aria-hidden="true"
                    ><span class="bar" style="--bar:0.00%"></span></span
                  ><strong>100 days</strong>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="mark-wrap" aria-hidden="true">
                <div class="marks">
                  <span style="--tick:0%">100 d</span><span
                    style="--tick:24.88%">1 yr</span
                  ><span style="--tick:69.09%">10 yr</span><span
                    style="--tick:100%">50 yr</span>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <figcaption>
              Logarithmic scale beginning at 100 days. Voyager’s row is
              observed. Other rows divide 25.9 billion kilometers by the stated
              cruise speed and omit acceleration, flybys, braking, and Earth’s
              motion. The illustrative speeds are comparison points.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
          <p>
            At this distance, a fly-through and a rendezvous are two different
            jobs. An instrument that samples interstellar plasma can keep going.
            A ship delivering cargo has to arrive at the right place and shed
            its speed. For a fast probe, the relevant question is what
            scientific payload can survive the trip and transmit its data. For a
            Mars cargo ship, count the kilograms delivered and the time elapsed
            from departure to arrival, including the maneuvers at both ends.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="builders">
          <h2>4. Who’s building it?</h2>
          <h3>Electric propulsion has already flown</h3>
          <p>
            NASA’s Psyche spacecraft uses xenon Hall thrusters and a
            solar-electric chassis built by Maxar Space Systems, now <a
              href="https://investors.intuitivemachines.com/news-releases/news-release-details/intuitive-machines-completes-acquisition-lanteris-space-systems"
              >Lanteris Space Systems, part of Intuitive Machines</a
            >. After its 2026 Mars flyby, Psyche resumed thrusting toward its
            asteroid. Electric thrusters can get more momentum change out of
            each kilogram of propellant than chemical engines, but they produce
            low thrust and need time to build up speed. Solar power also drops
            with the square of distance. At Jupiter, each square meter of panel
            receives about one twenty-seventh the sunlight it gets near Earth. <a
              href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/psyche/spacecraft/"
              >NASA’s Psyche spacecraft guide</a
            > · <a
              href="https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/psyche/2026/10/22/pedal-to-the-metal-nasas-psyche-heads-toward-asteroid-belt/"
              >October flight update</a
            >.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="https://www.adastrarocket.com/">Ad Astra Rocket Company</a
            >, founded by former NASA astronaut Franklin Chang Díaz, is working
            on higher-power plasma propulsion. Its VASIMR test engine ran for 88
            continuous hours at 80 kilowatts in a ground test. The next question
            for this class of engine is how to supply sustained power and reject
            heat with a spacecraft light enough to accelerate appreciably. A
            fast exhaust stream helps conserve propellant. The ship’s
            acceleration still depends on how much thrust that stream produces
            and how much mass it has to move. <a
              href="https://www.adastrarocket.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/2021_AIAA_ASCEND_VASIMR.pdf"
              >Ad Astra’s AIAA test report</a
            >.
          </p>
          <h3>Fission could keep the thrusters on farther out</h3>
          <p>
            NASA and the U.S. Department of Energy are building <a
              href="https://www.nasa.gov/mission/space-reactor-1-freedom/"
              >Space Reactor-1 Freedom</a
            >, a nuclear-electric demonstration aimed at a 2028 launch. Idaho
            National Laboratory is developing the reactor and <a
              href="https://www.sierralobo.com/">Sierra Lobo</a
            > is supplying the machinery that turns reactor heat into electricity.
            That electricity drives the thrusters. The engineering question for faster
            trips is how much electrical power a flight system can deliver for each
            kilogram of reactor, shielding, power conversion gear and radiators.
            <a
              href="https://www.nasa.gov/centers-and-facilities/glenn/sr-1-freedom-advances-with-progress-on-nuclear-power-module/"
              >NASA’s October hardware update</a
            >.
          </p>
          <p>
            There’s also nuclear thermal propulsion. A reactor heats propellant
            directly, trading some of electric propulsion’s propellant
            efficiency for much higher thrust. <a
              href="https://www.ga.com/fission-energy-systems/nuclear-thermal-propulsion"
              >General Atomics Electromagnetic Systems</a
            > is developing and ground-testing fuel technology for that approach.
            This could help with high-thrust departure or arrival burns. For a light-day
            fly-through, the useful measure is the final escape speed after the vehicle
            has carried and expelled its propellant. <a
              href="https://www.gao.gov/assets/gao-25-107591.pdf"
              >GAO’s assessment of NASA’s propulsion portfolio</a
            >.
          </p>
          <h3>Fusion teams are testing pieces of an engine</h3>
          <p>
            Richard Dinan’s UK company <a href="https://pulsarfusion.com/"
              >Pulsar Fusion</a
            > reported first plasma in its Sunbird exhaust test rig in March 2026.
            In California, <a href="https://www.helicityspace.com/"
              >Helicity Space</a
            > is developing a pulsed fusion propulsion concept with co-founders Setthivoine
            You, Marta Calvo and Stephane Lintner, and investment from Lockheed Martin
            Ventures. A useful fusion rocket has to sustain useful output, direct
            it into thrust, and carry the equipment that makes it work. Sunbird’s
            reported milestone established that the test rig made plasma. A measured
            fusion-powered thrust run and a viable flight mass are further milestones.
            <a
              href="https://www.militaryaerospace.com/uncrewed/news/55372912/pulsar-fusion-reaches-first-plasma-milestone-in-sunbird-fusion-propulsion-system"
              >Sunbird test coverage</a
            > · <a href="https://www.helicityspace.com/aboutus"
              >Helicity’s team</a
            > · <a href="https://www.helicityspace.com/news"
              >Helicity’s investment announcement</a
            >.
          </p>
          <h3>Sails can leave the power source behind</h3>
          <p>
            Sunlight can push a sail without propellant. NASA has deployed an
            80-square-meter sail in orbit, and <a
              href="https://redwirespace.com/">Redwire</a
            > and <a href="https://www.nexolve.com/">NeXolve</a> built deployment
            hardware and sail film, respectively, for a much larger NASA ground test
            led by a Marshall team that included Les Johnson. A close pass by the
            Sun could give a lightweight sail a much stronger push. One 2026 analysis
            estimated that an ideal sail passing within 0.05 AU of the Sun could
            reach 105 km/s if the spacecraft and sail together weighed about five
            grams per square meter of sail. That’s a demanding mass budget before
            accounting for what survives the heat. <a
              href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.04198"
              >Solar-sail trajectory analysis</a
            >.
          </p>
          <p>
            A laser sail replaces sunlight with a concentrated beam. <a
              href="https://breakthroughinitiatives.org/initiative/3"
              >Breakthrough Starshot</a
            >, launched by Yuri Milner and Stephen Hawking, studied accelerating
            gram-scale probes to about 20 percent of light speed. At that cruise
            speed, one light-day takes five days. The project went on hiatus in
            2025 without a launch. Caltech’s team led by Harry Atwater measured
            laser radiation pressure on a tiny tethered sail in the lab. A
            free-flying probe would need a sail that stays centered in the beam
            and survives the heating, plus a way to point back and communicate
            across the distance. <a
              href="https://www.nasa.gov/technology/nasa-solar-sail-technology-passes-crucial-deployment-test/"
              >NASA’s sail deployment work</a
            > · <a
              href="https://www.caltech.edu/about/news/the-pressure-to-explore-caltech-researchers-take-first-experimental-steps-toward-lightsails-that-could-reach-distant-star-systems"
              >Caltech’s lab result</a
            > · <a
              href="https://engineering.wisc.edu/news/smooth-sailing-how-optics-and-photonics-could-propel-probes-to-outer-space/"
              >University of Wisconsin’s Starshot update</a
            >.
          </p>
        </section>
        <p>
          There’s a credible route to beating Voyager by decades for a small
          probe. Getting a heavier spacecraft there in a few years is a
          different power and mass problem. And the achievement we should try to
          repeat is the part that happens at the end. A spacecraft launched in
          1977 is still taking measurements in a place we’d never been. If we
          send a faster one after it, give it something worth measuring and a
          radio we’ll still be listening for when it gets there.
        </p>
